WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.010
Across the UK, we take it for granted

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:05.550
that if a fire breaks out
you can call an emergency number,

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:06.550
firefighters will arrive,

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:08.990
they'll do their best to save lives
and put out the fire,

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:11.860
and you will not get sent a bill
for making that call.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:15.790
Sure, if some movie director has arranged
a massive pyrotechnic stunt for a movie

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:19.020
and they need firefighters standing by
just in case something goes wrong,

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:23.010
they'll probably pay for the time there,
but in everyday life,

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:28.070
firefighters are funded by government,
through taxes, for everyone.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:29.830
But that's not always been the case.

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:34.710
The Great Fire of London in 1666
lasted for four days.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.960
The idea of "firefighters" hadn't
really been invented at that point.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.550
The Great Fire was only stopped
by a change in the wind,

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:44.340
and by the military blowing up houses
to create a firebreak.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:46.440
The whole of central London
burned to the ground.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.570
Rebuilding took 30 years.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:51.390
The enormous monument on the site
where the fire started

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:55.210
is still standing today,
350 years later.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:58.120
After the Great Fire, the job
of fire protection in London

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.489
was taken up by insurance companies.

00:01:00.489 --> 00:01:02.430
Building owners would pay a fee
for a few years' coverage,

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:05.569
and the insurance company would
attach a metal "fire mark"

00:01:05.569 --> 00:01:07.109
to the front of the building.

00:01:07.109 --> 00:01:08.109
Like...

00:01:08.109 --> 00:01:10.749
...these, on the wall of St James' Palace.

00:01:10.749 --> 00:01:15.270
So in 18th-century London, if a fire
broke out and smoke started rising,

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:18.499
several fire brigades, each run by
a different insurance company,

00:01:18.499 --> 00:01:20.380
would all rush to the scene.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:21.899
And then they'd check for fire marks,

00:01:21.899 --> 00:01:25.200
and only fight the fire if they
saw their own company's mark,

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.540
or the mark of a company
they had an agreement with.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:31.359
If the building wasn't insured,
they would let it burn.

00:01:31.359 --> 00:01:34.009
Their job was protection
of property, not life.

00:01:34.009 --> 00:01:35.649
If a neighbouring building
was insured with them,

00:01:35.649 --> 00:01:37.609
and the fire looked to be
spreading that way,

00:01:37.609 --> 00:01:40.270
well, they'd make sure
that building was safe, of course.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:41.599
And it wasn't just London.

00:01:41.599 --> 00:01:42.599
There were similar schemes

00:01:42.599 --> 00:01:45.259
all around the country
and around the world.

00:01:45.259 --> 00:01:49.789
If firefighters standing aside while
a building burns seems immoral to you...

00:01:49.789 --> 00:01:52.249
well, there are lots of places
where that's still the case today.

00:01:52.249 --> 00:01:55.430
Or where firefighting companies
will send a bill afterwards,

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:58.009
assuming that an insurance company
will pay it.

00:01:58.009 --> 00:02:01.039
In places where there aren't enough people,
or there's not enough political will

00:02:01.039 --> 00:02:04.630
for a government-funded fire service,
that may still be the only option.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:08.729
In London, it wasn't until 1833
that ten of those insurance companies

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:12.790
joined their fire brigades together
to create a single fire service...

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:14.270
...still private.

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:17.920
In 1866, the government
finally took over and created

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.720
the London Fire Engine Establishment
as a public service.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.690
But that was only after another major fire.

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:28.810
It took disaster,
it took death and destruction,

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:31.990
to move things forward to the point
where you can call the fire service

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:36.769
to save lives, not just property,
and without having to worry about the bill.

00:02:36.769 --> 00:02:38.190
You can still see a few fire marks

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:41.680
on old buildings around London
and around the UK these days,

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.390
although not all of them are original,

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:45.310
some are recent reproductions
put up for effect.

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:49.209
Heck, those on the wall of the Palace
might be reproductions for all I know.

00:02:49.209 --> 00:02:50.840
But fortunately, it doesn't matter,

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:55.670
because firefighters don't have
to check for them any more.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:58.640
That was going to be a really good
shot of Buckingham Palace

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:00.810
and all you're going to see
is the traffic light.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.541
Don't know why I thought
I'd be able to get

00:03:03.541 --> 00:03:06.563
a good zoom shot of
Buckingham Palace from there.

00:03:07.409 --> 00:03:08.409
The road's closed, by the way.

00:03:08.409 --> 00:03:09.409
I'm just going to make that clear.

00:03:09.409 --> 00:03:11.575
I didn't just walk out into, uh,
an active road. It's...

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:13.260
It's closed off for pedestrians.

